sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-18> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The membranous cups (loricas) of Salpingoeca, Tintinnus and similar protozoa arise inevitably from substances adsorbed in the surface layer of the protoplasm, and molecular forces make the resulting pellicle a surface of least possible area under the circumstances, here an unduloid; this efficient cause, rather than a protective purpose, explains their form.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Protozoan loricas form by adsorption into least-area surfaces, not for protection";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter V, “The Forms of Cells”; topic “Physical versus teleological explanation”. Scope: loricate protozoa such as Salpingoeca, Tintinnus, Dinobryon and Codonella"
}
